people are really good at copying each  other's faces  in fact we're the best but Don expansion  to facial expressions gorillas and  orangutangs even copy those expressions  it's been thought though that this  attention to faces and copying of facial  expressions was a feature of social  creatures now researchers have shown  that son bears can also copy each  other's facial expressions exactly  something only ever seen before in  primates scientists studied a group of  22 bears living together at the Bornean  son bear conservation center they  recorded hundreds of voluntary play  sessions between different pairs of  bears  and found the Bears made two distinct  open-mouthed facial expressions and one  the bear simply opens its mouth and the  other the bear opens its mouth while  wrinkling its nose and burying its top  teeth having two different facial  expressions allow the researchers to  judge whether the Bears were mimicking  their partners faces exactly if the  Bears only made one face it would be  impossible to tell whether the  expression was being passed from bear to  bear or if it was just a face that Bears  made while playing the videos revealed  that 13 of the 22 Bears copied their  playmates facial expression exactly  within a second of seeing it the  researchers were surprised to find exact  facial mimicry in son bears because the  bears don't have much of a social life  in the wild the Bears also showed what's  called social sensitivity or the ability  to sense the attention of others  eighty-five percent of the time that a  bear was making a face another bear had  a front-row seat finding such  sophisticated communication in the  antisocial son bear makes the authors  think facial mimicry might be more  common than previously thought it also  suggests that social skills like  communication can be useful adaptations  even for animals with seemingly simple  social lives now what the Bears are  actually communicating to one another  there's not much data 